\f5\fs26 The blank was loaded into the coronial meat wagon and the two company operatives set about finding the best and most profitable place to deliver the dead body. There was not much money in processing the dead and consequently most of the mortuaries were publicly owned and operated. The few private institutions were attached to teaching hospitals that conducted scandalous experiments under the guise of research. No one had yet created a Frankensteinian abomination, but it did not stop them trying. They paid good money for a fresh body and the blank was a fresh as they came, but they had been re-directed to a public facility in another security zone and had spent most of the day arguing with customs officials.\
The blank was Emil Kalheri, a hard-working and decent man from a large family of builders and labourers. He was not wealthy, a level 1 could not be, but his family did not go without. He was just the kind of soft-hearted fuck-wit company agents could easily manipulate. When he was diagnosed with terminal testicular cancer, it had already metastasised, leaving three months of precious life. He knew he could not possibly gather together enough money to provide for his family, but he had a stroke of luck when he was contacted by Agents from Pre-emptive Reciprocity and Pro-active Euthanasia. His initial impulse to being recruited as an assassin was to recoil in revulsion and run in the opposite direction, but his family needed money and they were offering plenty. He only had to shoot a scum-bag manager (something most workers had fantasized about at one time or another) who, it was explained to him, was a prolific paedophile.\
It was early evening when Agent Jones arrived at the morgue, but Emil was waiting for him, laid out in the main necropsy room on one of the scores of examination tables used for autopsies, many already underway. Rows and rows of pathologists spoke quietly and precisely into microphones hanging from above while they hacked, sawed and sliced through bone, meat and sinew. They slopped whole organs and tissue samples into jars, under the gaze of trainee physicians who watched in both fascination and disgust, some barely keeping down their lunch, while autopsy technicians and assorted ghouls giggled in macabre amusement. The head Pathologist was laughing hysterically as she scuttled out of the necropsy room, pointing at the puking kid-doctors. \
Apart from his clothing, Emil had not a scintilla of personal property and it was now reasonably certain that the cleaner had possession of the disc. Agent Jones rightly figured that, in the ordinary course of events, this cleaner would not be too difficult to track down, but no matter how he interrogated the link, no matter what angle he took, nothing could be found. Nothing! He knew this to be impossible, the Intel-network had access to every data-base in the Dominion. Everyone left a footprint somewhere and most leave craters and divots everywhere, but he could make no headway with this fellow. There were very few people in the entire Dominion who were afforded this level of anonymity and it was an absolute certainty that none of them were low-life cleaning contractors. Agent Jones and his ilk, by necessity, skulked around in the shadows to accomplish their dastardly deeds and were non-existent in any public record. Was the cleaner a member of another clandestine organisation? If so, he was unaware of them, but that did not mean they did not exist.
